trong>82\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 77\u00b0F, and the record is 96\u00b0F (1903). The low of <strong>57\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Laredo has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Laredo's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Laredo has set <strong class=\"sig\">14 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 91 that chance alone would give, and <strong>117 record highs</strong>, more than the 91 that chance alone would give. The 1990s hold the most record highs of any decade (64). Laredo's all-time high is 115\u00b0F, on June 17, 1908.","t_chg":"Laredo's nights got warmer. Its days didn't.","p_chg":"Across 92 complete years: Laredo's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.37\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.29 to +0.45), about +4.5\u00b0F since 1903. Laredo's average high shows no clear change: +0.10\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.01 to +0.20) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero.","t_mon":"Lows rose in all 12 months. Highs rose in 1 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Laredo's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.08 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 18.9 inches a year. The wettest year was 1971 (42.28 in); the driest, 2011 (6.65 in). Laredo almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 11 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}